{"name":"Ficus","hex":"#3b593a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Ficus","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A taxonomic genus within the family Moraceae – figs and banyans."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Ficus"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ficus","blocks":[{"text":"Ficus is a genus of about 850 species of woody trees, shrubs, vines, epiphytes and hemiepiphytes in the family Moraceae. Collectively known as fig trees or figs, they are native throughout the tropics with a few species extending into the semi-warm temperate zone. Many Ficus species are grown for their fruits, though only two species, the common fig and sycamore fig, are cultivated to any extent, with common fig being the type species and by far the most important. The fruit of most other species are also edible though they are usually of only local economic importance or eaten as bushfood. However, they are extremely important food resources for wildlife. Figs are also of considerable cultural importance throughout the tropics, both as objects of worship and for their many practical uses."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Sycomoros_old.jpg/330px-Sycomoros_old.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ficus"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Ficus-AerialRoot.jpg/500px-Ficus-AerialRoot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Ficus-AerialRoot.jpg/1280px-Ficus-AerialRoot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Ficus-AerialRoot.jpg/500px-Ficus-AerialRoot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aerial roots that may eventually provide structural support","link":"https://en.wik